1. What is OOP in Java?

OOP = Object-Oriented Programming.

It is a programming paradigm where we organize software around objects, rather than primarily around functions.

An object contains:

State → data/properties
Behavior → methods/functions



2. Why do we need OOP?



This improves:

organization
maintainability
reusability
scalability
security
extensibility


3. The 4 Pillars of OOP

This is extremely important for interviews.

Remember:

EIPA

Encapsulation
Inhheritance
Polymorphism
Abstraction

4. Encapsulation

Encapsulation means:

Bundling data and methods together while restricting direct access to the internal state.


for example


class BankAccount {

    private double balance;

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

You cannot do:

BankAccount account = new BankAccount();

account.balance = -100000;


Because the class controls how its data is modified.

This is data hiding.


5. Inheritance

Inheritance allows one class to acquire properties and behavior from another class.

class Animal {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}
class Dog extends Animal {

    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}

Now:

Dog dog = new Dog();

dog.eat();
dog.bark();

Dog inherits eat() from Animal.







7. Polymorphism

Polymorphism means:

One interface/reference, multiple forms of behavior.

There are two major forms in Java:

Compile-time polymorphism

Method overloading

class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
}

Same method name:

add()

Different parameters.




Runtime polymorphism

Method overriding

class Animal {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}

Now:

Animal animal = new Dog();

animal.sound();

Output:

Bark

The method executed is determined at runtime.

This is one of the most important Java interview concepts.



8. Abstraction

Abstraction means:

Hiding implementation details and exposing only the necessary functionality.

Real-world example:

When you use:

car.start();

You don't need to know exactly how the engine, fuel injection and ignition system work.

Java provides abstraction mainly through:

abstract classes
interfaces

Example:

abstract class Vehicle {

    abstract void start();
}
class Car extends Vehicle {

    @Override
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Car starts");
    }
}

The user knows:

start()

but doesn't necessarily need to know its implementation.



10. What is a Class?

A class is a blueprint/template for creating objects.

class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    void study() {
        System.out.println("Studying");
    }
}

The class itself describes what a student object should contain.



11. What is an Object?

An object is an instance of a class.

Student student = new Student();

Here:

Student → class
student → reference variable
new Student() → object

This distinction is frequently tested.

Technically:

Student student;

doesn't create an object.

It creates a reference variable.

Object creation happens with:

new Student();





12. What is a Constructor?

A constructor is a special member of a class used to initialize objects.

Example:

class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

Creating an object:

Student student = new Student("Akshay", 31);

The constructor runs when the object is created.



----- IMPORTANT 13. Constructor rules — MEMORIZE THESE


A constructor:

1. Has the same name as the class
class Student {

    Student() {
    }
}
2. Has no return type

Correct:

Student() {
}

Incorrect:

void Student() {
}

The second one is a method, not a constructor.

3. Is automatically invoked during object creation
Student s = new Student();
4. Can be overloaded
Student() {
}

Student(String name) {
}

Student(String name, int age) {
}
5. Cannot be overridden

Constructors are not inherited, so they cannot be overridden.

6. Can have access modifiers
public Student() {
}

private Student() {
}

protected Student() {
}



14. Types of Constructors

No-argument constructor

class Student {

    Student() {
        System.out.println("Constructor called");
    }
}

Parameterized constructor

class Student {

    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

Default constructor

This is a very important distinction.

If you don't declare any constructor, Java provides a default constructor automatically.

class Student {

    String name;
}

Conceptually Java provides:

Student() {
    super();
}

But if you write:

Student(String name) {
    this.name = name;
}

Java does not automatically provide:

Student()

Therefore:

Student s = new Student();

will fail.



---- CONCEPT : SUPER


18. super

super refers to the immediate parent class.

Example:

class Animal {

    String name = "Animal";
}
class Dog extends Animal {

    String name = "Dog";

    void printNames() {
        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(super.name);
    }
}

Output:

Dog
Animal
19. super() in constructors

Very important.

class Animal {

    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Animal constructor");
    }
}
class Dog extends Animal {

    Dog() {
        System.out.println("Dog constructor");
    }
}

When:

Dog dog = new Dog();

Output:

Animal constructor
Dog constructor

Why?

Because the child constructor implicitly calls:

super();

unless another constructor invocation is explicitly provided.

20. Constructor execution order

Consider:

class A {

    A() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }
}

class B extends A {

    B() {
        System.out.println("B");
    }
}

class C extends B {

    C() {
        System.out.println("C");
    }
}
C c = new C();

Output:

A
B
C

Parent constructor executes before child constructor.




-- CONCEPT : SUPER





-- ACCESS MODIFIER

This is another very high-priority interview topic.

Java has four access levels:

| Access Modifier | Same Class | Same Package | Subclass (Other Package) | Other Package |
|-----------------|:----------:|:------------:|:------------------------:|:-------------:|
| `private`       | ✅         | ❌           | ❌                       | ❌            |
| `default`       | ✅         | ✅           | ❌                       | ❌            |
| `protected`     | ✅         | ✅           | ✅                       | ❌            |
| `public`        | ✅         | ✅           | ✅                       | ✅            |


private - Accessible only inside the same class.

default - It can be accessed by classes in the same package, but not from unrelated classes in another package.

protected - protected allows access: Within the same package, Through inheritance in another package

public - Accessible from anywhere where the class itself is accessible.





27. Method Overloading

Same method name but different parameter list.

class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

Overloading can differ by:

number of parameters
parameter types
order of parameter types



28. Method Overriding

Child class provides a new implementation of a parent method.

class Animal {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}

Important:

Animal a = new Dog();

a.sound();

Output:

Bark

This is runtime polymorphism.



32. Abstract Class

An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly.

abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound();

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

You cannot:

Animal a = new Animal(); // ERROR

But:

class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}

Then:

Animal a = new Dog();

is valid.

An abstract class can have:

constructors
instance variables
concrete methods
abstract methods
static members




33. Interface

An interface defines a contract.

interface Payment {

    void pay();
}

Implementation:

class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paid using credit card");
    }
}

Interface references are extremely important for abstraction and polymorphism:

Payment payment = new CreditCardPayment();

payment.pay();





34. Abstract Class vs Interface

Interview favorite.

Abstract Class	Interface
extends	implements
Can have instance variables	Fields are implicitly public static final
Can have constructors	Cannot have constructors
Can have concrete methods	Can have abstract, default, static, and private methods
A class can extend only one class	A class can implement multiple interfaces

Modern Java interfaces are more powerful than the simplistic "only abstract methods" definition.




Q1. What is OOP?

Answer:

OOP is a programming paradigm that organizes software around objects containing state and behavior. Java uses OOP concepts such as encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction to improve maintainability, reusability and scalability.

Why asked?

Tests whether you understand the fundamental programming model of Java.

Q2. What are the four pillars of OOP?

Answer:

Encapsulation
Inheritance
Polymorphism
Abstraction

Why asked?

This is one of the most fundamental Java interview questions.

Q3. What is encapsulation?

Answer:

Encapsulation is bundling data and methods together and controlling access to the internal state of an object, commonly using access modifiers such as private.

Why asked?

Tests understanding of data protection and class design.

Q4. What is abstraction?

Answer:

Abstraction hides implementation details and exposes only the essential functionality. In Java it is primarily achieved using abstract classes and interfaces.

Why asked?

Tests whether you understand how complex systems expose simple APIs.

Q5. Encapsulation vs abstraction?

Answer:

Encapsulation focuses on protecting and controlling access to data, whereas abstraction focuses on hiding implementation complexity and exposing essential behavior.

Why asked?

Extremely common conceptual question.

Q6. What is inheritance?

Answer:

Inheritance allows a child class to acquire accessible fields and methods from a parent class using extends.

class Dog extends Animal {
}

Why asked?

Tests understanding of reuse and IS-A relationships.

Q7. Does Java support multiple inheritance?

Answer:

Java does not support multiple inheritance through classes.

class C extends A, B // invalid

However, Java supports implementing multiple interfaces.

class C implements A, B {
}

Why asked?

Very common Java-specific question.

Q8. What is polymorphism?

Answer:

Polymorphism means one interface/reference can represent different implementations. Java supports compile-time polymorphism through method overloading and runtime polymorphism through method overriding.

Why asked?

Fundamental OOP concept.

Q9. Overloading vs overriding?

Answer:

Overloading:

same method name
different parameter list
generally resolved at compile time

Overriding:

child provides implementation of inherited method
same signature
resolved dynamically at runtime

Why asked?

One of the most frequently asked Java questions.

Q10. Can return type alone distinguish overloaded methods?

Answer:

No.

int test(int x)
double test(int x)

is invalid.

Why asked?

Tests whether you actually understand method signatures.

Q11. What is a constructor?

Answer:

A constructor is a special class member used to initialize an object. It has the same name as the class and has no return type.

Why asked?

Constructors are fundamental to object creation.

Q12. Constructor vs method?

Answer:

Constructor	Method
Initializes object	Performs behavior
Same name as class	Can have any valid name
No return type	Has return type or void
Invoked during object creation	Called explicitly/invoked
Cannot be overridden	Can be overridden
Q13. Can constructors be overloaded?

Answer:

Yes.

Student() {}

Student(String name) {}

Student(String name, int age) {}

Why asked?

Tests constructor understanding.

Q14. Can constructors be overridden?

Answer:

No.

Constructors aren't inherited, so they cannot be overridden.

Why asked?

Classic Java trick question.

Q15. What happens if you don't define a constructor?

Answer:

If the class declares no constructor, Java provides a default no-argument constructor.

But if you declare any constructor yourself, Java doesn't automatically add the no-argument constructor.

Why asked?

Extremely common constructor trap.

Q16. What is this?

Answer:

this refers to the current object.

It is commonly used to distinguish instance variables from parameters and to invoke another constructor using this().

Q17. What is super?

Answer:

super refers to the immediate parent class. It can be used to access parent members and invoke the parent constructor using super().

Q18. this() vs super()?

Answer:

this();

calls another constructor in the same class.

super();

calls a constructor of the parent class.

Both must appear as the first statement in a constructor.

Also, you cannot use both directly in the same constructor because both would need to be first.

Q19. What are access modifiers?

Answer:

Java has:

private
default/package-private
protected
public

They control where a class member can be accessed.

Q20. Which is the most restrictive access modifier?

Answer:

private.

It restricts access to the declaring class.

Q21. Which access modifier provides the widest access?

Answer:

public.

Q22. What is default access?

Answer:

When no access modifier is specified, the member has package-private access.

It is accessible within the same package.

Q23. Explain protected.

Answer:

protected members are accessible within the same package and are also accessible to subclasses outside the package, subject to Java's protected-access rules.

Why asked?

protected is frequently misunderstood.

Q24. Can a top-level class be private?

Answer:

No.

A top-level class can be:

public

or:

package-private

Nested classes can use private and protected.

Q25. Can a constructor be private?

Answer:

Yes.

class Singleton {

    private Singleton() {
    }
}

This prevents external code from directly creating objects.

Why asked?

Tests access modifiers plus constructor knowledge.

Q26. Can an abstract class have a constructor?

Answer:

Yes.

Even though you cannot directly instantiate the abstract class, its constructor executes when a concrete subclass object is created.

Q27. Can an abstract class have non-abstract methods?

Answer:

Yes.

abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound();

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}
Q28. Can an interface have constructors?

Answer:

No.

Interfaces cannot be instantiated and therefore don't have constructors.

Q29. Can a private method be overridden?

Answer:

No.

A private method is not inherited by subclasses, so it cannot be overridden.

Q30. Can a final method be overridden?

Answer:

No.

final prevents method overriding.

Q31. Can a final class be inherited?

Answer:

No.

final class A {
}

cannot be extended.

Q32. Can static methods be overridden?

Answer:

No.

Static methods belong to the class rather than participating in runtime overriding. A static method with the same signature in a child class is method hiding.

Q33. What is dynamic method dispatch?

Answer:

It is the mechanism by which Java determines the overridden instance method to execute at runtime based on the actual object.

Animal animal = new Dog();

animal.sound();

If Dog overrides sound(), the Dog implementation executes.

Q34. What is IS-A?

Inheritance.

Dog IS-A Animal
class Dog extends Animal
Q35. What is HAS-A?

Composition/aggregation relationship.

Car HAS-A Engine
class Car {
    Engine engine;
}
38. Interview Code-Tracing Questions

These are especially valuable because interviewers love making you predict output.

Question
class A {

    A() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }
}

class B extends A {

    B() {
        System.out.println("B");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        B b = new B();
    }
}
Answer
A
B

Because the child constructor implicitly invokes super() first.

39. Another very important one
class Parent {

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    @Override
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Child");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Parent p = new Child();

        p.show();
    }
}

Output:

Child

Why?

Because overridden instance methods use runtime polymorphism.

40. One more classic
class Parent {

    static void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    static void show() {
        System.out.println("Child");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Parent p = new Child();

        p.show();
    }
}

Output:

Parent

Why?

Because static methods are resolved using the reference type, not dynamically using the object type.

This is method hiding, not overriding.






